            "<strong>Surveillance</strong> is the ongoing, systematic collection, analysis and interpretation of health data, and its dissemination to those who need to act. It is not a register that is filled and filed; it is the mechanism by which one case of cholera in Ada becomes a district response the same day and by which a ward learns that three of its surgical patients this month had the same organism in their wounds. Two surveillance systems concern the nurse. <strong>HAI surveillance</strong> inside the facility — counting surgical site infections, catheter and line infections, ventilator pneumonia and resistant organisms against the number of procedures or device-days, by the IPC team, with results fed back to the ward that generated them. And <strong>public health surveillance</strong> under Ghana's <strong>Integrated Disease Surveillance and Response (IDSR)</strong> strategy, in which every facility, from CHPS compound to teaching hospital, reports priority diseases on standard case definitions: some <strong>immediately</strong> (within 24 hours, by the fastest means — telephone, SMS, WhatsApp — followed by the form), the rest <strong>weekly</strong> and <strong>monthly</strong>, up through the sub-district and district health management team to the region and the Ghana Health Service, landing in <strong>DHIS2</strong>, the national database. Community-based surveillance adds the volunteers, chemical sellers, traditional healers and teachers who report rumours and unusual events from where the first case actually appears.",
